      "name": null,
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y.$",
      "worked_solution": "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{y}^{2+1}}{2+1})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{3}}{6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
